## 1.  What is Spring Boot?

| Feature | What it means |
|---------|---------------|
| **Opinionated “starter” projects** | A set of pre‑configured Maven/Gradle “starters” (e.g. `spring-boot-starter-web`) that bring in only the libraries you need. |
| **Auto‑configuration** | The framework automatically wires beans based on what’s on the classpath and what you’ve declared, so you rarely need explicit XML or Java config. |
| **Embedded servers** | Comes with an embedded Tomcat (or Jetty/Undertow) so you can run your app as a stand‑alone JAR. |
| **Developer productivity** | Live‑reload (`spring-boot-devtools`), Actuator endpoints (`/actuator/health`, `/actuator/metrics`), and excellent Maven/Gradle plugins. |
| **Micro‑service ready** | Works seamlessly with Spring Cloud, Docker, Kubernetes, etc. |

In short: **Spring Boot lets you write “just‑enough” Spring code to get a production‑ready application up and running in minutes.**

---

## 2.  Quick‑start: Build a REST API with Spring Boot

Below is a **complete, minimal CRUD API** that manages a `Book` entity.  
The same pattern works for any domain model.

> **Prerequisites**
> * Java 17+ (or 11, 17, 21…)
> * Maven or Gradle
> * (Optional) Postman, curl or any HTTP client
> * (Optional) MySQL/PostgreSQL if you want persistence, otherwise use an in‑memory DB like H2

---

### 2.1  Create the Project

#### Using Spring Initializr

1. Go to <https://start.spring.io/>
2. Pick **Maven Project**, **Java**, **Spring Boot 3.3** (or latest LTS).
3. Project metadata:
   * Group: `com.example`
   * Artifact: `bookapi`
   * Name: `BookApi`
   * Description: `Demo REST API`
4. Add dependencies:
   * **Spring Web**
   * **Spring Data JPA**
   * **H2 Database** (in‑memory, handy for demos)
   * **Spring Boot DevTools** (optional)
   * **Spring Boot Test** (for unit tests)
5. Click **Generate** → unzip the downloaded ZIP.

> **Result** – a ready‑to‑run `BookApiApplication.java` and the directory layout you’ll see in the next steps.

#### Manual Setup (for Maven)

```bash
$ mkdir bookapi && cd bookapi
$ mvn archetype:generate -DgroupId=com.example -DartifactId=bookapi -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart -DinteractiveMode=false
$ cd bookapi
```

Add the following to `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- Spring Web -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Spring Data JPA -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- H2 in‑memory DB -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- DevTools (optional) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-devtools</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Test -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

